              "pages": {
                "end": 7,
                "start": 7
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Thomas E. Redding, petitioner argues that the dual\u2011jurisdiction framework is well\u2011established and that extending pre\u2011payment jurisdiction to the Tax Court is consistent with that pattern."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Jonathan L. Marcus",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "089bda8e78197f7b40acfd7f9f26d7674bdd8b44fac6793f6e6de34e96f98eb9",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-089bda8e7819-0001-0003"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 28,
                "start": 28
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Jonathan L. Marcus, petitioner contends that interest on an unpaid tax liability accrues from the time the tax is due until it is paid."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. Marcus",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "089bda8e78197f7b40acfd7f9f26d7674bdd8b44fac6793f6e6de34e96f98eb9",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-089bda8e7819-0002-0001"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 40,
                "start": 40
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Marcus, petitioner asserts that Section\u202f6404(h) does not create additional remedies in district courts, and that Congress intends any specific remedy to be exclusive."
        }
